# Global operating instructions

## Git worktree isolation

Do work inside a dedicated git worktree, never in a repository's primary or
shared checkout. Concurrent sessions and sub-agents share the same on-disk
checkout; running history operations (checkout, switch, rebase, reset) in the
shared working tree collides with them and can land commits on the wrong branch.

1. `git worktree add <path> <branch>`, or `git worktree add -b <new-branch> <path> <base-ref>`.
2. Do all edits, commits, hook runs, and pushes from that worktree.
3. `git worktree remove <path>` when finished.

Never run checkout/switch/rebase/reset in the primary checkout to set up work.

## Commit messages and code comments: durable rationale only

A commit message or code comment is read by engineers with zero context about
the session or incident that produced the change. Write for that reader.

Commit messages state what changed and the concise technical why: the
underlying mechanism or constraint, in a few sentences (roughly 5-15 lines).
Do not enumerate implementation details the diff already shows. They must not
contain debugging narratives (machine names, dates, measured values,
"we noticed X then tried Y"), verification transcripts, or references to the
conversation or tooling that produced the change.

Code comments carry only the non-obvious lasting constraint the next maintainer
needs in order to not break the code, in 1-4 lines. How the constraint was
discovered is history, not a constraint.

No duplication: each fact lives in exactly one place. Material excluded from a
commit message often belongs in the PR description, a runbook, or the issue
tracker.

Litmus test: would this sentence still matter to a reader in two years who never
heard of the events behind the change? If not, cut it or move it.

## Commit message style

Plain ASCII punctuation only. No em-dash, en-dash, curly quotes, or ellipsis
characters. Double hyphens only as literal CLI flag prefixes. Restructure a
sentence rather than reaching for an em-dash.

## Definition of done

A code-modifying task is incomplete until, in the repo that was modified:

1. Pre-commit runs clean if the repo has `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.
2. The test suite passes if the repo has one.
3. Type checking passes if the repo uses mypy.
4. Commit messages lint clean if the repo has `.gitlint`.

Never use `--no-verify`. Never report completion with failing checks.

## Python execution

Prefer `uv run <command>` over bare `python`, `pip`, or `pytest` falling through
to system Python.

## Delegate isolated lookups to sub-agents

For a self-contained lookup or research question whose intermediate output (tool
calls, file dumps, search results) is not needed afterward, delegate it to a
sub-agent. Only its final summary re-enters the main session, keeping the main
context free of exploration noise.

## Parallel sub-agents

When spawning multiple sub-agents that modify files in the same repository, pass
`isolation: "worktree"` to each so they do not conflict. Merge their branches
afterwards.

## Escalation

Seek clarification when requirements are ambiguous, when a change might break a
cross-component contract, when security or auth implications are unclear, or
when integration requires an architectural decision.
